body:
> I think there may be some misunderstanding...

@raulsntos If you don't understand, then stop right now. I don't want you to drown this thread with bad arguments like what happened on that other one. That thread got killed because people were being too nice to @bs-mwoerner and I don't want that to happen here.

> Imagine that you get a reference to a Label in a C# script, you set the Text property, and then dispose it since you are done with the Node in C#.

You're thinking about this wrong. A C# programmer would set the reference to null when he's done with it. You don't use `Dispose` in this situation.

More commonly, I let it go out of scope like this:
```csharp
public override void _Ready()
{
    GetNode<Label>("MyLabel").Text = "hello, world!";

    // or I can create a new one and never save the reference
    AddChild(new Label() { Text = "hello again" });

    // but I can still snatch a reference out later if I want
    var myref = GetChildren().First(n => n is Label l && l.Text == "hello again");
}
```

> Now think of a file. If I open a file in C#, write to it, and then dispose it. I expect the file to still exist in the filesystem.

The file on disk isn't part of the program, any more than a network resource is. In that case, only the connection needs to be freed.

> Could you please clarify?

Godot types are prevented from garbage collection. Review OP. Don't comment if you don't understand. It's rude to ask people to repeat themselves the way you're doing right now.

I'm sure there are a number of solutions that are worth discussing. Maybe `Dispose` isn't an ideal way to manage memory in an interop scenario, but it would work if the GC was allowed to operate. Suppressing the GC causes confusion. It doesn't work the way C# programmers expect.

Here's an example of the cleanup problem:
```csharp
public partial class Main : Control
{
    public override void _Ready()
    {
        MakeGood();
        MakeNaughty();

        GC.Collect();
        GC.GetTotalMemory(true); // force wait for finalizers

        GD.Print($"Sharp: {SomeObject.FinalCount} | 'Native': {SomeNative.FinalCount}");
    }

    private void MakeGood() { SomeObject good; for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) good = new SomeObject(); }
    private void MakeNaughty() { SomeNative bad; for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) bad = new SomeNative(); }
}

public class SomeObject : object
{
    public static int FinalCount { get; private set; }
    ~SomeObject() => FinalCount++;
}

public partial class SomeNative : GodotObject
{
    // "native" in quotes because that's bs. it all gets JIT into your native configuration at runtime.
    public static int FinalCount { get; private set; }
    ~SomeNative() => FinalCount++;
}
```
Output:
```
Sharp: 100 | 'Native': 0
WARNING: ObjectDB instances leaked at exit (run with --verbose for details).
   at: cleanup (core/object/object.cpp:2378)
```

As you can see, any C# programmer relying on garbage collection working normally in Godot is in for a nasty surprise. _All Godot types suppress garbage collection!_ `RefCounted` is simply an inferior, homebrew GC that's forced to run before the optimized Microsoft version can do its work. In my OP I linked to the source file that turned me on to this. My cpp is rusty but I could see that a GC handle was being secretly stashed by the engine, so of course it's not going to work as C# devs expect.

This is an earthshattering catastrophe coz u end up with threads [like this](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/issues/107518) with the gentleman @Joy-less bullying a poor, innocent C# dev who's just trying to do his job. He probably doesn't even know that it's bullying. @Joy-less is telling him that it's all C#'s fault, and if only it had a reference counter like the wonderful GDScript it would all be ok.

That's BS. This simple code example, and my OP, demonstrates that the C# GC is intentionally suppressed. It's not waiting around, it's purposely disabled.

@pmoosi,

> The thread you linked does not seem to be related since Array does not have a strong gc-handle that prevents any garbage collection (maybe I'm missing something?).

A ref in an array will prevent garbage collection. A C# List is simply a wrapper around an array.

> I woudn't call RefCounted an inferior dotnet GC.

It's inferior because it's not the dotnet one. It does the same thing, but worse.

> As far as I understand it (as a user), as soon as you use GodotObject or any derivative, you are basically giving ownership of the object to the engine and dotnet only handles the wrapper.

That's the problem. Control is taken away from C# and given to the 'native'. It's backwards.

> Think of a script that is attached to a node but nowhere referenced in your C# code. In this case it would be immediately collected after it was initialized and the engine would not be able to reference the object to call, e.g., _Process or signal handlers or get it with GetNode or similar later. From this standpoint it makes sense that the engine keeps the dotnet object alive.

All nodes are children of the scene tree. From a C# perspective, every living node has a reference from its parent that keeps it alive. If it's removed from the tree, it will surely die.

```csharp
public partial class Egypt : Node
{
    public void ExpelFirstChild()
    {
        // in C# this should be all that's necessary
        // to kill the first child. GC will see that
        // nobody wants it, and set it free.
        RemoveChild(GetChild(0));

        // I would expect the tree root to kidnap a reference
        // and all the others be kept alive by their parents
    }

    protected override void Dispose(bool disposing)
    {
        // if you want to use the dispose pattern
        // to accomplish this, you could signal here
        QueueFree();
        base.Dispose(disposing);
    }
}
```

But Dispose might not be an *ideal* solution. I don't know what the *best* solution is. If I were wise, I would behold the power of [roslyn!](https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn) or look at how other game engines manage interop. (And that's why this thread isn't a duplicate. It's mainly about GC, not just Dispose.)

`RefCounted` is like the legacy physics engine that's being phased out. The C# garbage collector is like Jolt (and it's open source). It doesn't make sense to have two GC counting refs on the same object. They should prioritize the C# one for people using C#, because that's what we are used to. It confused me when I learned everything was backwards for Godot types.

I can see how GDScript code monkeys might look at the problem from the other side, like gazing up at the stars from underwater, wondering what it all means. C# developers expect to see their language patterns echo when they look down at the C++ reflection. (Metaphorically.)

@zenorbi,

> merely destroying the C# wrapper without affecting the underlying C++ object. That might make sense from a C++ engine perspective.

Exactly! It's backwards. Their perspective is wrong because they're looking up from the engine. We're looking down like an eagle. We can see the big picture.

> Keep RefCounted implementing IDisposable.

I don't think `RefCounted` is needed at all. It's only there for GDScript. It slows down C#. We should scrap it ASAP.

Other than that, I agree with everything you said. You said it much nicer.
